{
  "grammar": "kotlin",
  "note": [
    "Read from tree-sitter-kotlin's scanner.c, which is itself a copy of",
    "tree-sitter-julia's. The memory is one stack pushed two bytes at a time -",
    "`{delimiter, prefix_len}` - and the census read that as a `marks` stack whose",
    "element is a kind and a count. This book claims the string family and the",
    "nested block comment; automatic-semicolon insertion, the import dot and the",
    "primary-constructor keyword are four keyword look-aheads whose every branch",
    "is gated on `valid_symbols`, so they belong to a caller that has a permission",
    "set and are out of the cohort here.",
    "",
    "`interp`, `brace` and `ident` are marks the C does not keep, and that is not a",
    "liberty: the C is not asked inside an interpolation because STRING_CONTENT is",
    "not in `valid_symbols` there, and rung 1 has no parse table to say so. They",
    "are the parser's own bracket stack, standing in for it, and they carry no",
    "answer - every rule that touches them emits nothing."
  ],
  "asks": "token",
  "cohort": [
    "_string_start",
    "_string_end",
    "string_content",
    "_interpolation_expression_start",
    "_interpolation_identifier_start",
    "multiline_comment"
  ],
  "kinds": {
    "single": 1,
    "triple": 2,
    "interp": 3,
    "brace": 4,
    "ident": 5
  },
  "probes": {
    "dollars": "\\$+",
    "interp_head_ahead": "(?=[A-Za-z_{])",
    "brace_ahead": "(?=\\{)",
    "empty_interp": "\\{\\}",
    "word_ahead": "(?=[A-Za-z_])",
    "word": "[A-Za-z0-9_]+",
    "open_brace": "\\{",
    "close_brace": "\\}",
    "open_single": "([ \\t\\r\\n\\f\\v]*)(\\$*)\"(?!\"\")",
    "open_triple": "([ \\t\\r\\n\\f\\v]*)(\\$*)\"\"\"",
    "single_body": "(?:\\\\\\$(?!\")|\\\\[^$]|[^\"\\\\$])+(?=[\"$])",
    "triple_body": "(?:\\\\\\$(?!\")|[^\"$])*\"{1,2}(?!\")|(?:\\\\\\$(?!\")|[^\"$])+(?=[\"$])",
    "escaped_dollar_end": "\\\\\\$\"",
    "single_end": "\"",
    "triple_end": "\"{3,}",
    "open_comment": "([ \\t\\r\\n\\f\\v]*)/\\*",
    "slash_star": "/\\*",
    "star_slash": "\\*/"
  },
  "rules": [
    {
      "name": "interpolated_identifier_ends",
      "note": "`$foo` hands the identifier to the parser, so content resumes where word characters stop - which is a test on the bytes at the offset, the same shape as a heredoc tag test, and not a length remembered from before.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "ident"
        ],
        [
          "no_probe",
          "word"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "pop",
          "marks"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "interpolation_brace_opens",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "in",
          [
            "interp",
            "brace"
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "open_brace"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "push",
          "marks",
          "brace",
          0
        ],
        [
          "abstain"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "interpolation_brace_closes",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "brace"
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "close_brace"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "pop",
          "marks"
        ],
        [
          "abstain"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "interpolation_closes",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "interp"
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "close_brace"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "pop",
          "marks"
        ],
        [
          "abstain"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "excess_dollars_are_content",
      "note": "scanner.c:140-147. In a `$$\"\u2026\"` string exactly two dollars interpolate; a third is literal, and the C answers with the *first* one alone and re-reads the rest on the next ask.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "in",
          [
            "single",
            "triple"
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "dollars"
        ],
        [
          "marks.top.count",
          "<",
          [
            "span",
            0
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "interp_head_ahead"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "emit",
          "string_content",
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "interpolation_expression_starts",
      "note": "scanner.c:152-165. `${}` is refused outright rather than answered zero-width, so the guard says the braces may not be empty.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "in",
          [
            "single",
            "triple"
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "dollars"
        ],
        [
          "marks.top.count",
          "=",
          [
            "span",
            0
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "brace_ahead"
        ],
        [
          "no_probe",
          "empty_interp"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "push",
          "marks",
          "interp",
          0
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_interpolation_expression_start",
          [
            "+",
            [
              "span",
              0
            ],
            1
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "interpolation_identifier_starts",
      "note": "scanner.c:166-170. The answer is the dollars only; the identifier behind them is the parser's.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "in",
          [
            "single",
            "triple"
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "dollars"
        ],
        [
          "marks.top.count",
          "=",
          [
            "span",
            0
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "word_ahead"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "push",
          "marks",
          "ident",
          0
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_interpolation_identifier_start",
          [
            "span",
            0
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "lone_dollars_are_content",
      "note": "scanner.c:173-179. Too few dollars, or a dollar followed by anything that cannot begin a name: all of them are literal.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "in",
          [
            "single",
            "triple"
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "dollars"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "emit",
          "string_content",
          [
            "span",
            0
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "escaped_dollar_closes_string",
      "note": "scanner.c:188-199, and the comment there calls it an edge case: `\"aa\\$\"` consumes the backslash, the dollar and the quote as one STRING_END. It is not gated on the delimiter being single, so a triple-quoted string closes on it too - transcribed as the C decides it, not as Kotlin means it.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "in",
          [
            "single",
            "triple"
          ]
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "escaped_dollar_end"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "pop",
          "marks"
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_string_end",
          3
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "triple_content",
      "note": "scanner.c:208-233. One or two quotes inside a triple-quoted string are content and are answered together with whatever came before them; three end it. `\\` is not an escape here, so it is ordinary content.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "triple"
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "triple_body"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "emit",
          "string_content"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "triple_closes",
      "note": "scanner.c:242-249: `if we see a bunch of quotes in a row, then we just go until they stop appearing`.",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "triple"
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "triple_end"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "pop",
          "marks"
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_string_end"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "single_content",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "single"
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "single_body"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "emit",
          "string_content"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "single_closes",
      "phase": "inside",
      "when": [
        [
          "marks.top.kind",
          "=",
          "single"
        ],
        [
          "probe",
          "single_end"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "pop",
          "marks"
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_string_end",
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "string_opens_triple",
      "note": "scanner.c:72-96. The leading dollars are the interpolation prefix and the mark carries how many; a string with none still interpolates on one, which is why the count floors at 1.",
      "phase": "opening",
      "when": [
        [
          "probe",
          "open_triple"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "skip",
          [
            "span",
            1
          ]
        ],
        [
          "push",
          "marks",
          "triple",
          [
            "max",
            1,
            [
              "span",
              2
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_string_start",
          [
            "-",
            "width",
            [
              "span",
              1
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "string_opens_single",
      "phase": "opening",
      "when": [
        [
          "probe",
          "open_single"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "skip",
          [
            "span",
            1
          ]
        ],
        [
          "push",
          "marks",
          "single",
          [
            "max",
            1,
            [
              "span",
              2
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "_string_start",
          [
            "-",
            "width",
            [
              "span",
              1
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "multiline_comment",
      "note": "scanner.c:269-323. Nested, so the extent is a balanced run rather than a pattern, and an unterminated one ends at end of input because the C decided to answer it rather than refuse.",
      "phase": "bounded",
      "when": [
        [
          "probe",
          "open_comment"
        ],
        [
          "nest",
          "slash_star",
          "star_slash"
        ]
      ],
      "then": [
        [
          "skip",
          [
            "span",
            1
          ]
        ],
        [
          "emit",
          "multiline_comment",
          [
            "-",
            "width",
            [
              "span",
              1
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    }
  ]
}
